d3d11: Say in the log what compiling cost.

A game that hitches the first time it draws something new is waiting for a shader or for a
pipeline, and nothing said so: DXMT counted none of it. Four things are now counted for the
process, each with its time: the shaders compiled from Direct3D's bytecode, the shaders read
from DXMT's shader cache instead, the pipelines Metal made of them (its own call, in the four
places a pipeline is made), and the draws and dispatches whose pipeline was not there yet when
the encoding thread came to them, which is where a frame waits. A swap chain says them with
its other lines, every half minute and when it goes: one line for the process, and only when
there was something to count.

  DXGI: compiles of the last 15 s (frame_cause_test.exe, 14:44:38): 4 shaders compiled (100
  ms), 0 from the shader cache (0 ms), 2 pipelines (134 ms), 2 draws waited for a pipeline
  (186.9 ms); so far 4 shaders compiled, 0 from the shader cache, 2 pipelines, draws waited
  186.9 ms

That is macos/tests/frame_cause_test.c, which has two shaders of its own and makes two more
and a pipeline that no run had before. Run again with the same salt, the earlier run's four
shaders come from the shader cache (1 ms for the four).

The times of the shaders and the pipelines are summed over the threads that compile, so
they can be more than the clock's; the draws' wait is the encoding thread's own. A pipeline
that is there costs its draw one test more, a compile two clock reads. The seconds are those
since the line before, or since d3d11.dll was loaded.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR DXMT. Applied by macos/build-dxmt.sh to
github.com/athei/dxmt at 01e59e9bde030f105937f249afaac92ada01b4d7, after 0001 to 0012.
Written with an AI assistant (Claude). DXMT does not accept contributions made or
co-authored by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be sent to it as a pull
request.
